PR #429  overall: compliant
Spec: FDPIC redefines gp as per-function state rather than global invariant; any kernel/firmware signal delivery, context switch, or trap handler that does not reload gp from the target function descriptor silently corrupts data accesses, enabling cross-object memory corruption or privilege escape via GOT misdirection
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/signal.c:413-481  setup_rt_frame
      Signal delivery sets regs->epc to raw sa_handler (line 468) without FDPIC function-descriptor dereference, and leaves gp unchanged (line 464 comment confirms 'Registers that we don't modify keep the value they had from user-space'). On FDPIC, the signal handler inherits the interrupted context's stale gp, pointing to the wrong library's GOT. All subsequent data accesses in the handler resolve against the caller's GOT instead of its own. ARM's setup_return explicitly reloads r9 (PIC register) from the function descriptor; RISC-V has no equivalent logic. Currently latent because elf_check_fdpic() is undefined for RISC-V and CONFIG_BINFMT_ELF_FDPIC is gated on !MMU, but the code defect is unconditional.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/signal.c:353-388  setup_sigcontext
      Saves full pt_regs (including gp) verbatim via __copy_to_user at line 362. The saved gp belongs to the interrupted context — possibly a different FDPIC library than the signal handler's. No FDPIC loadmap or descriptor information is annotated in the sigframe. On sigreturn, this saved gp becomes authoritative. The invariant that 'gp saved here is the gp that should be restored on sigreturn' holds for the interrupted context but is wrong if the handler were meant to change gp for its own execution. Depends on the implicit assumption that no FDPIC-aware handler exists.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/signal.c:219-279  restore_sigcontext
      Bulk-restores all GPRs including gp from sigframe via __copy_from_user (line 226). On sigreturn, gp is restored to the pre-signal value. This is architecturally correct for the interrupted context's continuation, but it silently discards any gp changes the handler should have made under FDPIC semantics. The handler ran with a wrong gp (from setup_rt_frame), and sigreturn overwrites even a corrected gp — masking the bug on return while execution was compromised during handler execution.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/compat_signal.c:199-243  compat_setup_rt_frame
      Identical gp-staleness bug as setup_rt_frame but for 32-bit compat tasks. Sets regs->epc to raw sa_handler (line 230) without FDPIC function-descriptor dereference. gp is left unchanged per the comment at lines 223-228. FDPIC 32-bit binaries in compat mode are equally affected. Same latent-but-confirmed status.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/compat_signal.c:159-174  compat_setup_sigcontext
      Converts pt_regs to compat_user_regs_struct via regs_to_cregs() (line 166), then copies verbatim. gp is translated but not validated or annotated with FDPIC loadmap context. Same implicit-invariant dependency as setup_sigcontext.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/compat_signal.c:102-117  compat_restore_sigcontext
      Restores gp via cregs_to_regs() from compat sigframe (line 111). Same silent-overwrite pattern as native restore_sigcontext: gp is restored to pre-signal value, masking the handler's gp corruption during execution.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/elf.h:69-74  ELF_FDPIC_PLAT_INIT
      ELF_FDPIC_PLAT_INIT sets a1=exec_loadmap, a2=interp_loadmap, a3=dynamic_addr but does NOT set gp from the executable's function descriptor. ARM's ELF_FDPIC_PLAT_INIT also does not set r9, but ARM compensates in start_thread and signal delivery — RISC-V does neither. Additionally, elf_check_fdpic() is NOT defined in this file (or anywhere in arch/riscv), so the generic weak fallback in fs/binfmt_elf_fdpic.c:117-118 returns 0 for all binaries, making the FDPIC loader path unreachable. Combined with CONFIG_BINFMT_ELF_FDPIC depending on (RISCV && !MMU) at fs/Kconfig.binfmt:61, FDPIC binaries cannot be loaded on MMU RISC-V kernels.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/elf.h:43-44  (missing) elf_check_fdpic
      RISC-V does not define elf_check_fdpic(). The generic weak fallback at fs/binfmt_elf_fdpic.c:117-118 returns 0 unconditionally, so no ELF binary is ever recognized as FDPIC on RISC-V. This is an infrastructure blocker: even if all signal-path gp fixes were implemented, the FDPIC loader would refuse to activate for any RISC-V binary. Without this, the FDPIC code path is unreachable.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:144-180  start_thread
      Initializes epc, sp, status for a new process but does NOT initialize gp. For FDPIC executables, gp must hold the executable's GOT base on entry. ARM's start_thread preserves r7-r9 for FDPIC personalities (via ELF_FDPIC_PLAT_INIT and explicit r9 saving). Without gp init, the RISC-V dynamic linker inherits a random/uninitialized gp on exec, which would cause all GOT-relative accesses to resolve to arbitrary addresses.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/ptrace.c:40-46  riscv_gpr_get
      Blindly copies full user_regs_struct including gp to userspace via membuf_write. A debugger reading regs when the target is in FDPIC library A captures gp=GOT_A, then writing those regs back when the target is executing in FDPIC library B corrupts gp to point at GOT_A. All subsequent GOT-relative data accesses in library B resolve to library A's data segment. GDB inferior calls across library boundaries are the primary exploit vector.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/ptrace.c:48-57  riscv_gpr_set
      Blindly writes full user_regs_struct including gp into pt_regs via user_regset_copyin. PTRACE_SETREGSET with a stale gp value captured from a different FDPIC library silently corrupts the target's GOT base pointer. No validation, no FDPIC-aware guard, no check that the written gp matches the current execution context's expected GOT.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:238-286  copy_thread
      Copies parent pt_regs wholesale including gp at line 268 (*childregs = *(current_pt_regs())). This is correct for fork() from an FDPIC parent (child inherits the same gp = same GOT base in the same library), but has no explicit FDPIC awareness. If clone() with CLONE_VM but a different execution context (e.g., entering a different shared library) were supported, gp would be stale. Depends on the implicit invariant that clone always enters the same FDPIC object as the parent.
