PR #429  overall: potential_risk
Spec: FDPIC redefines gp as per-function state rather than global invariant; any kernel/firmware signal delivery, context switch, or trap handler that does not reload gp from the target function descriptor silently corrupts data accesses, enabling cross-object memory corruption or privilege escape via GOT misdirection
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:545-577  TRAP_SAVE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_SP_T0
      Macro unconditionally saves gp at line 549 into trap_context alongside all GPRs on every trap entry. In FDPIC, gp is a per-function-GOT anchor, not a global invariant. This snapshots whichever module's gp was active at trap time. Currently safe because OpenSBI domains are non-FDPIC flat binaries, but the code encodes the non-FDPIC assumption that gp is ordinary architecturally-preserved state with no per-module semantics. If FDPIC domains are ever introduced, this is the save-side of gp confusion across every trap path.
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:614-645  TRAP_RESTORE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_A0_T0
      Macro unconditionally restores gp at line 618 from trap_context on every trap exit. Does not check whether the execution target (mepc) has been redirected to a different FDPIC module since the trap was taken. If sbi_trap_redirect or SSE injection changed mepc to point into a different binary, the restored gp still points to the original trapped module's GOT. The restore-side mirror of the FDPIC gp confusion vulnerability. Currently safe under non-FDPIC assumptions.
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:728-761  _reset_regs
      Zeros gp at line 735 during cold/warm boot. On warm-boot resume from non-retentive suspend, gp is set to 0. If the domain resume path resumes mid-function (not via crt0 which would reinitialize gp), the domain executes with gp=0, causing all GOT-relative accesses to dereference GOT[0] instead of the domain's actual GOT. The exploit chain: non-retentive suspend -> __sbi_hsm_suspend_non_ret_save skips gp -> warm-boot -> _reset_regs zeros gp -> sbi_hart_switch_mode does not reload gp -> domain resumes with gp=0.
  [OK] include/sbi/sbi_trap.h:129-205  sbi_trap_regs
      Struct defines unsigned long gp at line 140 as index-3 in the GPR array within the anointed struct. This is a faithful architectural mirror of the RISC-V register file layout. The struct itself is not the vulnerability — it is neutral plumbing. The vulnerability lives in the code that blindly saves gp into this field and restores it without cross-module awareness. Any FDPIC fix would need to touch this struct (e.g. adding a separate target_gp field or annotating gp as FDPIC-sensitive), but the struct per se is correct for current non-FDPIC OpenSBI.
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:673-690  _trap_handler
      Concrete instantiation of the blind gp save/restore pattern for non-H-extension harts. Calls TRAP_SAVE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_SP_T0 (saves gp) at line 678, dispatches to C handler (which may redirect mepc or inject SSE), then calls TRAP_RESTORE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_A0_T0 (restores stale gp) at line 684 before mret. No fence or check between the C handler's mepc modification and the gp restore. Any FDPIC fix must ensure gp is updated at this specific entry point when mepc has diverged from the original trap site.
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:695-724  _trap_handler_hyp
